// THE COLLECTIVE READING. The script is the operator's: /home/briansrls/lil/nccl_test.py
// (sha256 702a2bce83cb6c76ca51699457822fb06e840c37ba377bb0b5247a8b2653b1cc, 644 bytes, mtime
// 2026-11-08 19:14:49Z), run under torchrun with two ranks; it all-reduces 16 KiB, 256 KiB, 4 MiB and
// 64 MiB, 50 reps below 4 MiB and 20 at or above, printing per size "allreduce <KiB>: <us> busbw=<GB/s>"
// with busbw in decimal GB/s.
//
// THE COLLECTIVE READING, TAKEN FIRST-HAND at the operator's request on 2026-11-08 between 22:22:31Z and
// 22:23:02Z. Both arms ran the operator's own script (path and sha256 carried per row) in a short-lived `--rm`
// container with both CDI GPUs, NCCL_DEBUG=INFO and NCCL_DEBUG_SUBSYS=INIT,P2P,SHM,NET; the container was gone
// when the run printed its last line and nothing was left behind (checked: no laneA-* container). The two
// serving replicas were loaded the whole time (93,606 MiB used on each card, utilization sampled between 0 and
// 95 percent), so these are numbers from a busy machine, not a quiet one -- which is why the comparison below
// carries an obligation rather than being closed as settled (`srv1_collective_read_obligation`).
//
// WHAT THE RUN SAID, AND WHAT THE RELAYED LIST SAID. The relayed 16 KiB (~50 us, 0.3 GB/s) and 64 MiB
// (~5.2 ms, 13 GB/s) rows that came in the lane brief are corroborated inside a few percent by the default arm
// below (52.6 us / 0.31 GB/s, 5.39 ms / 12.45 GB/s), and the size behind the truncated 12.7 GB/s figure is the
// 4 MiB row (342.7 us, 12.24 GB/s) -- both are now ReadByThisSession rows, so no cell of the sweep is left
// unread and the relayed copies are gone rather than kept side by side. THE ONE CLAIM THAT DID NOT SURVIVE: the
// brief relayed that the default arm and NCCL_P2P_DISABLE=1 "behave the same". They do not. NCCL printed the
// same transport in both arms (every channel "via SHM/direct", "Check P2P Type isAllDirectP2p 0 directMode 0
// isAllCudaP2p 0"), yet disabling P2P costs about half the bandwidth from 256 KiB up: 4.79 to 1.79 GB/s at
// 256 KiB, 12.24 to 5.17 GB/s at 4 MiB, 12.45 to 5.83 GB/s at 64 MiB. Both arms were read seconds apart, and
// the faster arm ran while the Max-Q card was the busier of the two, so contention does not explain the
// difference away; what it does mean is that this is one pair of runs on a loaded machine, and the difference
// is recorded as a reading plus a hypothesis (`why NCCL_P2P_DISABLE=1 costs about half the bandwidth...`) rather
// than as the last word.
fn nccl_arm_instrument(arm: NonEmptyStr, port: NonEmptyStr, printed: NonEmptyStr) -> NonEmptyStr {
  string_concat_join(
    fields: [
      "docker run --rm --network host --ipc host --shm-size=2g --device nvidia.com/gpu=GPU-7b58568e-8bc9-4e4b-bc6c-c414b08936c3 --device nvidia.com/gpu=GPU-96b749dc-e2ea-5b6f-2e45-ee868b1a9750 -v /home/briansrls/lil:/lil:ro -e ARM=",
      arm as String,
      " -e MASTER_ADDR=127.0.0.1 -e MASTER_PORT=",
      port as String,
      " -e NCCL_DEBUG=INFO -e NCCL_DEBUG_SUBSYS=INIT,P2P,SHM,NET --entrypoint torchrun 2ad6245e058d --nnodes=1 --nproc-per-node=2 --master_addr=127.0.0.1 --master_port=",
      port as String,
      " /lil/nccl_test.py on srv1 (image 2ad6245e058d, torch 2.14.0a0+4fdf77b940.nv26.08, CUDA 13.4; rank 0 was cuda 0 = GPU-7b58568e, rank 1 cuda 1 = GPU-96b749dc). Both serving replicas loaded (93606 MiB each). Printed line: ",
      printed as String,
    ],
    separator: "",
  ) as NonEmptyStr
}

